@@ -0,0 +1,178 @@
+# Mid-cycle plan changes
+
+## Why
+
+Customers cannot change plan today. Support has to cancel and resubscribe them by hand.
+The billing package needs a self-serve plan change: upgrades and downgrades, optionally
+together with a switch between monthly and annual billing. It must be prorated so that
+nobody pays twice for the same days, and it must never pay money back.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+- `BillingService.change_plan(subscription_id: str, new_plan_code: str, new_interval: Interval | None = None) -> Invoice | None`
+  in `src/billing/service.py`. `new_interval=None` keeps the subscription's current interval.
+- Return the invoice issued for the change (recorded in the store like every other invoice),
+  or `None` when nothing is billed at the moment of the request.
+- Add `InvalidPlanChange(BillingError)` to `src/billing/errors.py` for change requests that
+  can't be honoured; keep using the existing error classes where they already fit.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Change date**: the service clock's `today()` when `change_plan` is called.
+- **Period days (d)**: calendar days in the current period, `period_end − period_start`.
+  That is 28–31 for a monthly period and 365/366 for an annual one.
+- **Remaining days (r)**: `period_end − change date`. The change date counts as remaining;
+  `period_end` is excluded.
+- **Target**: each request states the full target, a plan plus an interval. With
+  `new_interval=None`, the target interval is the subscription's current interval.
+- **Upgrade / downgrade**: decided only by comparing the plans' `monthly_price`,
+  whatever the interval. A higher monthly price is an upgrade and a lower one is a
+  downgrade. Equal monthly prices (including the same plan) are neither.
+- **Scheduled change**: a target stored on the subscription that takes effect at the next
+  renewal. A subscription has at most one.
+
+## Behaviour
+
+### Active subscription
+
+| Request | At the request | At the next renewal |
+|---|---|---|
+| Upgrade, same interval | Plan switches now; proration invoice returned | New plan billed at full price |
+| Upgrade + interval switch | Plan switches now; proration invoice at the **current** interval's prices returned; interval switch scheduled | New period of the new interval, billed at its full price |
+| Downgrade (with or without interval switch) | Scheduled; returns `None` | Moves to the target plan/interval, billed at full price |
+| Same monthly price, different plan (with or without interval switch) | Scheduled; returns `None` | Moves to the target, billed at full price |
+| Same plan, interval switch only | Scheduled; returns `None` | New period of the new interval, billed at its full price |
+| Target equals current plan + interval, a change is scheduled | Scheduled change dropped; returns `None` | Renews unchanged |
+| Target equals current plan + interval, nothing scheduled | `InvalidPlanChange` | — |
+
+### Trialing subscription
+
+Every change (upgrade, downgrade, interval switch) applies immediately and in full. No
+invoice is issued and the call returns `None`. The trial end date does not move. When the
+trial ends, the customer is billed the full price of the plan and interval they are on at
+that point.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Upgrades (active subscription)
+
+1. An upgrade issues one invoice dated on the change date. It belongs to the subscription
+   and its customer, is recorded in the store (it appears in `invoices_for`), and is the
+   value `change_plan` returns.
+2. The invoice has exactly two lines:
+   - a credit: `−round(old_price × r / d)`
+   - a charge: `+round(new_price × r / d)`
+
+   Both prices are the plans' list prices for the subscription's **current** interval.
+   Each amount is computed exactly (no rounded daily rate) and rounded half-up to cents on
+   its own.
+3. The invoice total is the sum of the two rounded lines, not the rounded net difference.
+4. The two-line rule holds even when a line is 0.00, for example when upgrading from a
+   free plan.
+5. After the upgrade the subscription is on the new plan right away. Status,
+   `period_start`, `period_end` and interval are unchanged.
+6. The next renewal, on the usual `period_end`, bills the new plan at full price for the
+   interval in effect.
+7. An upgrade applied now discards any previously scheduled change. The one exception is an
+   interval switch scheduled by the same request.
+8. An upgrade is always prorated from the plan the subscription is on at the moment of the
+   request. A scheduled change has not happened yet and is ignored for proration.
+9. Several upgrades in one period are each prorated from the then-current plan over the
+   days remaining on their own change date.
+
+### Scheduled changes (active subscription)
+
+10. Downgrades, equal-monthly-price plan changes and all interval switches (in either
+    direction, including monthly → annual) bill, refund and credit nothing at the request.
+    `change_plan` returns `None`, no invoice is recorded, and the subscription's current
+    plan, interval and period stay the same.
+11. A new request replaces whatever was scheduled before; the latest request wins. A request
+    with `new_interval=None` targets the current interval, so it also replaces a previously
+    scheduled interval switch.
+12. At the next renewal a scheduled change takes effect: the new period starts on the old
+    `period_end`, its length matches the target interval, and it is billed at the target
+    plan's full price for the target interval. The scheduled change is then cleared.
+13. When `renew_due` catches up several missed periods, a scheduled change applies to the
+    first of them only. Later periods continue on the new plan and interval.
+14. A scheduled change still takes effect if its target plan is retired after the request.
+
+### No money back
+
+15. `change_plan` never produces an invoice with a negative total, never refunds, and never
+    creates a credit balance. Any change that would give money back is scheduled for the
+    period end instead.
+
+### Trials
+
+16. During a trial, any valid change switches the plan and/or interval immediately, returns
+    `None` and records no invoice. `period_start` and `period_end` (the trial end) do not
+    change.
+17. When the trial ends, `renew_due` bills the full price of the plan and interval the
+    subscription is on at that point.
+
+### Retired plans
+
+18. Moving to a retired plan raises `PlanNotAvailable`.
+19. A customer on a retired plan may move off it like anyone else.
+20. A customer on a retired plan may switch interval while staying on that plan; it is
+    scheduled like any other interval switch.
+
+### Errors and rejected requests
+
+21. An unknown subscription id raises `SubscriptionNotFound`.
+22. A canceled subscription raises `SubscriptionNotActive`.
+23. An unknown plan code raises `PlanNotFound`.
+24. A target equal to the current plan and interval, with nothing scheduled, raises
+    `InvalidPlanChange`.
+25. A request made when the current period has already ended (`change date >= period_end`,
+    the renewal or trial end is due but `renew_due` has not run) raises
+    `InvalidPlanChange`. The caller runs renewals first.
+26. `InvalidPlanChange` is a subclass of `BillingError`, defined in `src/billing/errors.py`
+    and exported from the `billing` package like the other errors.
+27. A rejected request changes nothing: no invoice is recorded and the subscription
+    (including any scheduled change) is left as it was.
+28. Cancelling a subscription discards any scheduled change.
+
+### Existing behaviour
+
+29. `subscribe`, `renew_due` and `cancel` behave exactly as before for subscriptions that
+    have never had a plan change. The existing test suite passes unchanged.
+
+## Worked examples
+
+Basic is 10.00/month and 100.00/year, Pro is 30.00/month and 300.00/year (the test
+fixtures). Premium is 75.00/month and is assumed for the first two examples only.
+
+| Case | Period | Change date | r / d | Lines | Total |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Pro → Premium, monthly (Basic scheduled on Mar 5, requested on Mar 11) | 2026-03-01 → 2026-04-01 | 2026-03-11 | 21 / 31 | −20.32, +50.81 | **30.49** (a rounded net difference would give 30.48) |
+| Pro → Premium, monthly, last day | 2026-03-01 → 2026-04-01 | 2026-03-31 | 1 / 31 | −0.97, +2.42 | 1.45 |
+| Basic → Pro, annual | 2026-01-15 → 2027-01-15 | 2026-07-01 | 198 / 365 | −54.25, +162.74 | 108.49 |
+| Basic → Pro, monthly, on period start | 2026-01-15 → 2026-02-15 | 2026-01-15 | 31 / 31 | −10.00, +30.00 | 20.00 |
+
+In the first example the scheduled downgrade to Basic is discarded. The renewal on
+2026-04-01 bills Premium at 75.00.
+
+## Decisions made on the implementer's side (you said "don't mind")
+
+- A plan change with the same monthly price is scheduled for the period end (criterion 10).
+- A customer on a retired plan may switch interval on it (criterion 20).
+- A scheduled change whose target is later retired still applies (criterion 14).
+- A request after the period has ended is rejected rather than triggering a renewal
+  (criterion 25).
+- Cancelling discards a scheduled change (criterion 28).
+- An upgrade from a free plan keeps a 0.00 credit line (criterion 4).
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Refunds, credit notes, negative invoices, and customer credit balances.
+- Immediate interval switches, and prorating across intervals.
+- Proration for downgrades, or anything that would lower what the customer has already paid.
+- Changing trial length or the dates of the current period.
+- How a scheduled change is stored, or exposing it through a particular API. The
+  implementer chooses field names and representation, subject to the store contract and
+  frozen-dataclass conventions in the README.
+- Wording of invoice line descriptions.
+- Rescheduling or cancelling a scheduled change other than through `change_plan` (or
+  `cancel`).
+- Taxes, coupons, multi-currency, and quantity- or seat-based pricing.
